          %title -algebras in quantum mechanics
          %h1#algebras_in_quantum_mechanics
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi JBW
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} JBW
            \-algebras in quantum mechanics
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#motivation"} Motivation
              %li
                %a{:href => "#description"} Description
              %li
                %a{:href => "#dynamics"} Dynamics
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            It can be nice to describe the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kinematics"} kinematics
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+system"} quantum system
            using a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/JBW-algebra"} JBW-algebra
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dynamics"} dynamics
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "#dynamics"} below
          %h2#motivation Motivation
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi JBW
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} JBW
            \-algebras are arrived at by the confluence of several lines of motivation.
          %p
            To begin with, the algebraic approach to quantum mechanics (which is also the basis of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
            using
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+spaces"} phase spaces
            (or even
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifolds"} Poisson manifolds
            or even
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+algebras"} Poisson algebras
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            using
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+spaces"} Hilbert spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+observables"} algebra of observables
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+algebra"} commutative algebra
            underlying a Poisson algebra (or its
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complexification"} complexification
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+operators"} algebra of operators
            on a Hilbert space. This also allows one to apply
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superselection+rules"} superselection rules
            at a fundamental level by restricting to a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subalgebra"} subalgebra
          %p
            More particularly, one may restrict to
            %em bounded
            observables, that is those for which there is a largest possible absolute value that could be observed. There is a philosophical reason for this, that no measuring device in reality is unbounded; but it also makes the algebra more tractable, and an arbitrary observable
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi O
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O
            can still be described by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequence"} sequence
            of bounded observables with increasing cut-offs.
          %p
            At this point, it's natural to expect the algebra of observables to be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+space"} Banach space
            (and so, being an algebra, some kind of
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+algebra"} Banach algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/norm"} norm
            giving this largest possible absolute value. In fact,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+number"} complex
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^*
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebra"} algebras
            are usually used; both the algebra of bounded
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+functions"} continuous functions
            on a Poisson manifold (or indeed any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+compact+topological+space"} locally compact
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+space"} Hausdorff space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bounded+operators"} bounded operators
            on a Hilbert space are
            = succeed "-algebras." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^*
          %p
            Then we may further refine from
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^*
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi W
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W^*
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/W%2A-algebra"} algebras
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^*
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+functions"} continuous functions
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi W
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W^*
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+functions"} measurable functions
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/characteristic+functions"} characteristic functions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+subset"} measurable subset
            of phase space. (If it is on the boundary, then we won't be able to tell; this is reflected in that the
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi W
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W^*
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/almost+equality"} equal almost everywhere
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+calculus"} functional calculus
            of
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi W
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W^*
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
            of an observable with a measurable function on its
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
            = succeed "-algebra." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi W
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W^*
            = succeed "-algebra." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi W
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W^*
          %p
            There is, however, an objection to all of these algebras of observables, which is that they have many elements that are not observable. Only the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-adjoint+elements"} self-adjoint elements
            of these algebras are considered to be observable. (Even if we allow for complex-valued observables, only the
            = succeed "s" do
              %span.newWikiWord
                normal element
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/normal+element"} ?
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jordan+algebra"} Jordan algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+algebra"} commutative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative
            (and specifically we get the classical algebra of real-valued observables immediately). Thus, the algebra of observables is a Jordan algebra, even if it is viewed as embedded within a larger algebra.
          %p
            The analytic structure on a Jordan algebra that makes it the algebra of self-adjoint elements in a
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi W
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W^*
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/JW-algebra"} JW-algebra
            (Jordan
            = succeed "-algebra," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi W
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W^*
            = succeed ")" do
